  {/* ══════════════════════════════════════ 7 — built for speed */}
  <section id="speed" class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="speed-title">
    <h2 id="speed-title">Built for speed</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">
      Every claim here is about the architecture and can be checked in the
      source. No benchmark figures are published yet; when they are, they go here.
    </p>
    <div class="tri">
      <div class="tri-item tri-engine">
        <h3>One compiled binary</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li>Go compiles to a single static executable</li>
          <li>One production dependency — the Postgres driver</li>
          <li>API and admin in one process, no second server</li>
          <li>No plugin loading at runtime: a module you did not import is not in the binary</li>
          <li>Money as integers — no float arithmetic on the hot path</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="tri-item tri-store">
        <h3>Server-rendered storefront</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li>SvelteKit renders product and category pages on the server</li>
          <li>The first byte is HTML, not an empty shell</li>
          <li>Small client footprint by design</li>
          <li>Crawlers and answer engines see the catalogue</li>
          <li>Backend switch is a package change, not a rewrite</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="tri-item">
        <h3>One database hop</h3>
        <ul class="tick">
          <li>PostgreSQL, directly, through pgx</li>
          <li>No cache tier required to start</li>
          <li>48 append-only migrations, applied on boot</li>
          <li>A schema you could query yourself</li>
          <li>Add Redis or search only when a real need asks</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══════════════════════════════════════ 8 — real operations */}
  <section id="operations" class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="ops-title">
    <h2 id="ops-title">Built for real operations</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">Not weekend experiments. The depth shows in the workflows that already exist, not in the adjectives.</p>
    <ul class="evidence">
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-shield"/></svg><b>Roles and rights</b><span>52 distinct rights gate the admin and API, read and write separately, per resource.</span></li>
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-braces"/></svg><b>API keys</b><span>Scoped keys for integrations, using the same rights the admin does.</span></li>
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-bag"/></svg><b>Orders</b><span>Payment state and fulfillment state tracked separately, because they are separate facts.</span></li>
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-warehouse"/></svg><b>Inventory</b><span>Stock held per location, so a multi-warehouse store does not fake it with one number.</span></li>
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-card"/></svg><b>Payments</b><span>Nine provider modules. Money is integer minor units, never a float.</span></li>
      <li><svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#i-return"/></svg><b>Idempotent writes</b><span>Repeat a request safely; an importer that fires no events and moves no stock.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

