  {/* ══════════════════════════════════════ 11 — agent friendly */}
  <section id="agents" class="section wrap" aria-labelledby="agents-title">
    <h2 id="agents-title">Built for developers — and their coding agents</h2>
    <p class="section-lede">The repository is written to be read by a machine as well as a person. Claude, Codex, Cursor and Antigravity all pick up the same files; none of this implies any partnership with them.</p>
    <div class="tri">
      <div class="tri-item"><h3>Rules that are written down</h3><p><code>AGENTS.md</code> carries 18 architectural rules, and <code>skills/</code> carries 16 task guides, so an agent loads the one matching its job instead of reading the whole repository.</p></div>
      <div class="tri-item"><h3>A graph small enough to hold</h3><p>One production dependency and a flat package layout. Comments explain <em>why</em>, because the code already says what.</p></div>
      <div class="tri-item"><h3>A suite that catches the agent</h3><p><b>1,036 tests.</b> This is what makes agent-assisted change safe rather than merely fast: a wrong edit fails here, not in a customer's checkout.</p></div>
    </div>
    <ol class="agentflow">
      <li>Clone</li><li>Read <code>AGENTS.md</code></li><li>Modify a module</li><li><code>docker compose up</code></li><li>Tests catch it</li><li>Deploy</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="fineprint">Separately, the MCP module exposes a running store over the Model Context Protocol. Every tool calls the same domain service a REST request would — an agent never touches the database — and <code>ReadOnly</code> withholds every mutating tool while you are deciding how far to trust it.</p>
  </section>

